WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.846 --> 00:00:05.026
In the process plant the product is made that sits in the tank of line 3.

2
00:00:06.032 --> 00:00:10.687
A process plant is built from fixed steps, such as pumping, heating and mixing.

3
00:00:11.462 --> 00:00:13.447
They are called unit operations.

4
00:00:14.446 --> 00:00:16.153
Four topics follow the product.

5
00:00:16.907 --> 00:00:19.485
Two are about making it and letting it flow.

6
00:00:20.493 --> 00:00:23.500
The other two are about counting and about capacity.

7
00:00:24.831 --> 00:00:27.873
The first topic is Process types and diagrams.

8
00:00:28.623 --> 00:00:31.607
In the kettle you make one lot at a time, a batch.

9
00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:36.023
From the tank the product flows non-stop to the filling unit,

10
00:00:36.198 --> 00:00:38.009
because the cartons keep coming.

11
00:00:38.994 --> 00:00:43.882
For process types we often use a block diagram and a process flow diagram.

12
00:00:44.588 --> 00:00:48.594
In it every unit has a code, so everyone means the same unit.

13
00:00:49.953 --> 00:00:53.134
The second topic is Process variables and control.

14
00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:55.641
Four variables run the plant.

15
00:00:56.427 --> 00:00:58.877
They are pressure, temperature, flow and level.

16
00:00:59.885 --> 00:01:01.336
You keep them at their value,

17
00:01:01.476 --> 00:01:04.854
so every carton in the filling unit gets the same product.

18
00:01:05.857 --> 00:01:11.546
For measuring we often use a Coriolis meter for flow and a Pt100 for temperature.

19
00:01:12.268 --> 00:01:14.865
The Coriolis meter measures mass directly.

20
00:01:16.094 --> 00:01:18.973
The third topic is Mass and energy balances.

21
00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:24.008
What goes in, like the sacks on the pallet, comes out or stays in.

22
00:01:25.003 --> 00:01:27.847
If something is missing in the sum, look for a leak.

23
00:01:28.567 --> 00:01:32.155
Product can, for instance, disappear down the floor drain.

24
00:01:33.147 --> 00:01:35.910
For balances we often use a mass balance.

25
00:01:36.622 --> 00:01:38.421
It shows where product goes.

26
00:01:39.762 --> 00:01:42.572
The fourth topic is Batch capacity and scale-up.

27
00:01:43.311 --> 00:01:47.409
A cycle runs from one start to the next, and the waiting counts.

28
00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.729
What works in a flask often fails in the big kettle.

29
00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:54.142
So you scale up in steps.

30
00:01:55.142 --> 00:01:57.789
For scale-up we often use a pilot plant.

31
00:01:58.596 --> 00:02:00.976
There you test small what must later run big.

32
00:02:01.992 --> 00:02:06.346
The product is ready and goes through the pipe to the filling unit in hall B.

33
00:02:07.666 --> 00:02:08.839
This is the whole map.

34
00:02:09.577 --> 00:02:13.966
Process engineering basics is the third station of Making things.

35
00:02:14.991 --> 00:02:16.686
Every topic has four levels.

36
00:02:17.677 --> 00:02:21.137
Start with Process types and diagrams at Basics level.

37
00:02:21.848 --> 00:02:24.031
That lesson shows how a plant runs.

38
00:02:25.126 --> 00:02:29.085
At Working knowledge level you read drawings and control loops,

39
00:02:29.271 --> 00:02:31.117
work with energy and scale up.

40
00:02:32.147 --> 00:02:34.248
At Advanced level come buffer tanks,

41
00:02:34.411 --> 00:02:38.358
cascade control, standardising and scheduling several products.

42
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.274
At Expert level it is about start-up losses,

43
00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:46.209
less variation, pinch analysis and removing bottlenecks.

44
00:02:47.226 --> 00:02:51.290
Every lesson and every film in this module has a place on this map.
